# Insert-mode Ctrl-O runs one normal-mode command and comes back to
# INSERT (`:help i_CTRL-O`): after `A`, `Ctrl-O 0` puts the cursor on
# column 1 and the mode chip still reads INSERT. In NORMAL, Ctrl-O is the
# jumplist. Neither opens the file picker — that chord is VS Code's.
write b.txt "hello world\n"
open b.txt
command editor.use_vim
key A
expect screen contains " INSERT "
expect screen contains "Col 12 "
key ctrl+o
key 0
expect screen lacks "┌ Open file"
expect screen contains " INSERT "
expect screen contains "Col 1 "
key esc
expect screen contains " NORMAL "
key ctrl+o
expect screen lacks "┌ Open file"
expect screen contains " NORMAL "
